<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>AppIcon</string>
    <!--
      TCC usage descriptions. Each key below exists for the same reason: macOS
      attributes a privacy request from a child process to its *responsible
      process* — for everything Silo runs, that is Silo itself — and renders
      the responsible app's usage string in the consent prompt. With no string
      there is nothing to prompt with, so macOS silently denies instead of
      asking. The failure surfaces as a tool that "just doesn't work" (a
      capture producing 0 bytes, EHOSTUNREACH) with no permission dialog ever
      shown and no entry in System Settings to toggle.

      Silo is a host for arbitrary user processes, so it must carry a string
      for every service a terminal, agent, or dev server might reach — the same
      set VS Code ships. Services that are not Info.plist-gated (Screen
      Recording, Accessibility) need no key; macOS supplies their text.

      Each string here is only half of a grant: the matching hardened-runtime
      entitlement in Entitlements.plist is what permits the access, while the
      string is what lets macOS ask for it. Both are required; either one
      alone still fails. That file deliberately carries no comments and no
      non-ASCII characters - codesign parses entitlements with
      AMFIUnserializeXML, a stripped-down parser that rejects input which
      `plutil -lint` accepts, so a comment there breaks the signing step in
      release CI and nowhere else. Rationale for the entitlement set belongs
      in this comment instead.
    -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Silo needs local network access so terminals, agents, and dev servers you run inside it can reach services on your network.</string>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Silo needs microphone access so terminals, agents, and tools you run inside it can record audio.</string>
    <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Silo needs audio capture access so terminals, agents, and tools you run inside it can record system audio.</string>
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Silo needs camera access so terminals, agents, and tools you run inside it can capture video.</string>
    <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Silo needs to send Apple Events so terminals, agents, and tools you run inside it can automate other apps.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
